    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID54",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_2ade9986523ba7154e801079__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Avoiding stereotypes: Using different adjectives for women and men\n\nSometimes in English different adjectives are used to describe the same feature in women and men. There are also some words which – despite not having an explicit gender – have strong connotations that are strongly associated with only women or men.\n\nThis stems from the fact that some character traits, such as being ambitious, are considered by society attractive in men but negative in women.\n\nIt is not always easy to spot when adjectives are promoting gender stereotypes. The examples in the table below show some words to look out for and to avoid using to describe women.\n\n&gt; Tip!\n&gt;\n&gt; When describing a characteristic of a woman, ask yourself, ‘would I ever use this word to describe a man?’ (and vice versa) If not, then look for a term without gender connotations.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Different adjectives are sometimes used to describe the same feature in women and men, reflecting social judgments that treat traits such as ambition as attractive in men but negative in women. Words without explicit gender can still carry strong gender associations and promote stereotypes. Checking whether a descriptive word would also be used for a man can help identify gendered language and guide the choice of a term without gender connotations.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_2ade9986523ba7154e801079__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
