    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID58",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_ed17dae48efe72056ff2dfc1__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Stereotypes: How language manifests gender-related stereotypes\n\nStereotypes are generalised images about people within a society. A gender stereotype is a preconceived idea where women and men are assigned characteristics and roles determined and limited by their gender.\n\nStereotypes about gender often take one of two forms. One assumes all members of a category (such as a profession) share a gender, for example the assumption that all company directors are men and all secretaries are women. The other is assuming that all members of a gender share a characteristic, for example believing that all women love to shop or that ‘boys don’t cry’.\n\nThese stereotypes hurt people of all genders by placing expectations on what people should be.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes assign women and men preconceived characteristics and roles determined and limited by their gender. They can operate by assuming that everyone in a profession shares a gender or that everyone of a particular gender shares a characteristic. These stereotypes harm people of all genders by imposing expectations about what they should be.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_ed17dae48efe72056ff2dfc1__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
