      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.1 casual use of gendered slurs, profanities, and insults – Using gendered slurs, gender-based profanities and insults, but not to intentionally attack women. Only terms that traditionally describe women are in scope (e.g. ‘b*tch’, ‘sl*t’).</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Online gender-based hate speech includes manifestly offensive, indecent, or obscene messages; threatening, abusive, or insulting words or behaviour; insulting a person directly or in public; and imposing sexual or sexist comments or behaviour. Such conduct can harm reputation, bring a person into disrepute, put them at risk of losing confidence, and create an intimidating, hostile, or offensive situation. These patterns are relevant to gendered slurs, profanities, and insults because they describe offensive and sexist speech and the hostile environment it can produce.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
