    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID102",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_409ecc7e977c40bbbf8d2982__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Where does sexism come from?\n\n&gt; ### Definition: gender stereotypes\n&gt;\n&gt; Preconceived ideas whereby females and males are arbitrarily assigned characteristics and roles determined and limited by their gender.\n&gt;\n&gt; Source: EIGE.\n\nGender stereotypes underpin sexist behaviour and practices. Gender stereotypes do not need to be hostile to be harmful. Many workplace realities have been shaped by the belief that women and men have complementary characteristics, for example that men are individualistic and dominant, while women are caring and collaborative.\n\nHowever, stereotypically masculine characteristics are frequently valued more highly than stereotypically feminine characteristics (examples given below), although there is evidence this is changing. This is also true in the work context, where professional development books and programmes frequently urge women to stop displaying stereotypically feminine behaviour if they want to advance in their careers.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes assign characteristics and roles to females and males based on gender, and these stereotypes underpin sexist behaviour and practices. They can be harmful without being hostile. Workplace realities have been shaped by the belief that women and men have complementary characteristics, such as men being individualistic and dominant while women are caring and collaborative. Stereotypically masculine characteristics are often valued more highly than stereotypically feminine ones, and professional development materials frequently urge women to stop displaying stereotypically feminine behaviour if they want to advance in their careers.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_409ecc7e977c40bbbf8d2982__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
        "DataVisibility": {
