        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.3 backhanded gendered compliments – Ostensibly complimenting women, but actually belittling or implying their inferiority. This could include, but is not limited to: reduction of women’s value to their attractiveness or sexual desirability, or implication that women are innately frail, helpless or weak.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gendered terms are sometimes used as insults, and expressions that paint the feminine as negative treat being feminine as meaning weak or ineffective. Calling a handshake “ladylike” implies weakness, and telling someone they “throw like a girl” implies doing something badly, silly, or weakly. Using gender stereotypes in this way is sexist because it associates femininity with inferiority or ineffectiveness.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
