    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID134",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_6a51d7e45bed5de684e00632__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">2. SOCIETAL CONTEXT AND ROOT CAUSES OF CYBER VIOLENCE AND HATE SPEECH ONLINE AGAINST WOMEN &gt; 2.1.2. Normalisation and invisibility of online cyber violence against women\n\nBeyond the continuum of violence against women, there are other societal causes contributing to women’s victimisation online. Cyber violence is often presented by the media as being: a) a gender- neutral phenomenon; and b) a phenomenon of an individual matter resulting from of women’s naïveté/responsibility. Women are generally advised to “not feed the troll”, to “change their privacy settings” or to “go offline for a while”. This both reflects and contributes to cyber violence being normalised while covering up the victims’ perspectives.\n\nFrom research carried out in 2017 about the way British media frame cyber violence, it becomes clear how the legitimisation and normalisation of violence in media reports framed public opinion, debate and action, and implicitly victim blame(d) via “silencing strategies”. Advice given to victims of cyber harassment to not “feed the troll” actually calls upon victims not to challenge or resist “abusive (i.e. sexist, racist or misogynist) language and attitudes”. Moreover, the authors of the study argue that the way media report on trolling is “silencing victims” by under-reporting and normalising “trolling events” such as “rape threats, death threats, and body shaming, (...) and the advice given to victims on how they should respond to online abuse”. The authors recall that rape culture on the internet is prevalent and “incorporates aspects of popular misogyny, and entails anti-female violent expression via the threats of rape and death (or bodily harm) directed at women online. Expressions of aggressive male sexuality are eroticised in the online sphere (...) and in the press”.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Media representations frame online cyber violence against women as gender-neutral and as an individual problem caused by women’s naivety or responsibility, advising women not to “feed the troll,” change privacy settings, or go offline. Such advice normalizes abuse, silences victims, and shifts blame to women by discouraging them from challenging sexist, racist, or misogynist language and attitudes. Media under-reporting and normalization of trolling events such as rape threats, death threats, and body shaming, alongside prevalent internet rape culture and anti-female violent expression, understate and implicitly justify mistreatment of individual women.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_6a51d7e45bed5de684e00632__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
